# SYNOPSIS

```usage
vx rc-oracle [case ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`vx rc-oracle` checks our rc against 9front's.
Each case is a script `shell/rc/tests/oracle/<case>.rc`.
The command runs every case, or the cases named, through 9front's rc and through rcport(1), our rc on Linux, each in a fresh empty directory under `.build/rc-oracle/work/`, with an empty standard input and a limit of 20 seconds.
The two must agree in what they write to standard output, in their exit status, and in whether they write anything to standard error.

A case whose first line is `#flags: -e -x` runs with those flags in both shells.
The environment variable `rcoracle` holds the shell being run, its words separated by byte 1 as rc's environment keeps lists, so that a case may run its shell again.

9front's rc is built when it is missing or older than a source: the C files of `tools/rc-oracle/9front` are compiled by `clang` into `.build/rc-oracle/rc`, run with the `rcmain.unix` beside them.
rcport is built with `swift build --product rcport`.

The cases that differ are named in `shell/rc/tests/oracle/known.tsv`, each with a kind and a reason: `port`, a gap in our rc, or `linux`, an artefact of running on Linux.
A differing case that is not named fails, and so does a named case that now agrees, so the file stays exactly what is left.
The command prints a line for each failure, and at the end `vx: rc-oracle: <n> cases, <n> agree, <n> known divergences (<n> port, <n> linux)`.
It exits 1 if any case failed.

To add a case, write a script that prints what it checks, deterministically, with no timing races and no output that depends on the machine; run `vx rc-oracle <case>`; and either fix rc or name the case in `known.tsv` with its reason.

# FILES

: `shell/rc/tests/oracle/*.rc`
  The cases.
: `shell/rc/tests/oracle/known.tsv`
  The known divergences: case, kind and reason, separated by tabs.
: `tools/rc-oracle/9front`
  9front's rc, whose C files are built for Linux.
: `.build/rc-oracle/`
  The built shell and each case's work directory, with both shells' output.

